\title{The Transform}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-20}

\p{
    Take a [public-coin](dhsorens-0053) protocol in which the prover sends #{a_1}, receives a
    uniform challenge #{c_1}, sends #{a_2}, and so on through #{\mu} challenge rounds. Fiat–Shamir
    sets #{c_i = H(x, a_1, \dots, a_i)} for a hash function #{H} and a statement #{x}, and the proof
    is the string #{\pi = (a_1, \dots, a_{\mu+1})}. The verifier recomputes every #{c_i} from #{\pi}
    and runs the interactive verifier's decision predicate unchanged. Deployed implementations chain
    instead of re-hashing — #{c_i = H(c_{i-1}, a_i)}, which is what a [duplex sponge](dhsorens-004Z)
    computes — and the two agree as long as the chain is injective on transcripts.
}

\p{
    Public-coin is not a convenience here, it is the precondition. A private-coin verifier's
    challenges depend on randomness the prover must not learn, so there is no transcript to hash
    them out of; this is the first of several reasons [the distinction is
    load-bearing](dhsorens-0053) rather than presentational. An [IOP](dhsorens-0056) is not directly
    compilable either, because its verifier queries oracles the proof string does not contain — the
    [BCS transform](dhsorens-002H) realises those oracles as commitments first, and Fiat–Shamir is
    applied to the interactive argument that results. [Compilation](dhsorens-004S) is the two steps
    together.
}

\p{
    What must be in the hash is everything the verifier's decision depends on, and the requirement
    is easier to state than to satisfy. The statement #{x}, the public parameters, and every prior
    prover message all belong there; omitting the statement gives the \em{weak} variant, which is
    unsound whenever the prover chooses what to prove, and which [several dozen implementations
    shipped](dhsorens-0050). The serialisation matters for the same reason a Merkle tree's encoding
    does: two distinct transcripts that encode to one byte string are a collision the security proof
    assumed away.
}

\p{
    The transform hands the prover exactly one new power: it can retry. Nothing forces it to accept
    the first challenge it sees — it computes #{c_i} itself, and if the resulting challenge is
    unhelpful it changes #{a_i} and computes again. [State-restoration soundness](dhsorens-004M) is
    the notion that assumes precisely this adversary, and [proof-of-work grinding](dhsorens-003A) is
    the countermeasure that prices each attempt. The compilation loss is then whatever the
    interactive hypothesis supports: #{Q^\mu} from plain soundness, #{(Q+1)\kappa} from [special
    soundness](dhsorens-004K), linear in #{Q} from [round-by-round soundness](dhsorens-004L).
}

\p{
    Zero knowledge comes out of the same construction, by a mechanism worth separating from
    soundness. The simulator does not have a witness; it produces a transcript backwards, choosing
    the challenge first and \em{programming} the oracle to return it. That is a power the simulator
    has only because #{H} is idealised — a real hash cannot be reprogrammed — so honest-verifier
    zero knowledge of the interactive protocol upgrades to zero knowledge of the compiled one in the
    [random oracle model](dhsorens-004V) and by no argument that survives instantiation.
}

\p{
    Two alternatives exist and neither is deployed for succinct arguments. Fischlin's transform
    makes the prover produce, across many parallel repetitions, transcripts whose hashes have a
    prescribed number of leading zeros; the work of finding them forces the prover to expose enough
    distinct challenges per repetition that a straightline extractor can read a witness off the
    proof, with no rewinding and no #{Q}-dependent loss. It buys that with prover work exponential
    in the grinding parameter, a larger proof, and hypotheses — small challenge space, unique
    responses — that no [IOP-based](dhsorens-002E) system satisfies. Unruh's transform plays the
    same role in the [quantum](dhsorens-0051) setting. Both are formalised as case studies in
    [VCV-io](dhsorens-0042); neither appears in a zkVM.
}

\p{
    In [ArkLib](dhsorens-0040) the transform is defined and largely unproved, which is itself
    informative. \code{OracleReduction/FiatShamir/Basic.lean} builds \code{Prover.fiatShamir},
    \code{Verifier.fiatShamir}, and \code{Reduction.fiatShamir} by replacing each challenge with a
    query to a Fiat–Shamir oracle over the transcript so far, and states the three properties the
    transform should preserve: completeness, state-restoration soundness implying soundness, and
    honest-verifier zero knowledge implying zero knowledge. \code{fiatShamir_completeness} carries a
    \code{sorry} and the soundness implication is a \code{TODO}. The [soundness
    notions](dhsorens-002F) themselves are proved and their implications are theorems; the step that
    connects them to a non-interactive object is the one still open, in Lean as in the literature.
}
